rence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\nWe are given the three terms of the ODE as power series with the $n=0$ term isolated in each.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }9((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 12x{y}^{′} & =12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }12(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (9x-2)y & =-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(9{a}_{n-1}-2{a}_{n}){x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we continue from this point.\n\n**Step 1**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+12\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-2{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (9{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +12\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 9{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can determine a solution. Solving the indicial equation for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{\\lambda }^{